(2622426, 357042, 18219, '<td valign="top">this is great.<br/>looks like it has been post processed already, but obviously hasn''t.<br/>It did take me a couple of views before noticing the head in his hand.<br/>I thought it was a frying pan to start with :-)<br/>excellent idea and execution (no pun).</td>', 'this is great.looks like it has been post processed already, but obviously hasn''t.It did take me a couple of views before noticing the head in his hand.I thought it was a frying pan to start with :-)excellent idea and execution (no pun).', '2006-07-14 14:30:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(2623294, 356497, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>Straight from the Camera.  Dark.  Um.  <br/> <br/>You have an excellent composition here and lovely colors as well. (And I''m a sucker for those rolled haybales that seem to flow so nicely into the countryside)<br/> <br/>A Critique Club member is supposed to suggest ways to improve your image, and yes, I caught that note that you work 12-hour shifts.  <br/> <br/>But still, it''s the lighting that needs to be improved for this image to work perfectly.  Many photographers wait hours for that perfect moment that will make their image The Perfect One.  So without post-processing and without the ability to wait for the perfect moment, well, you did pretty darn well with this very nice image.<br/> <br/>I''d love to see you revisit this image someday in the right light.  You''ll have a winner, for sure.  (And hey, it''s right across the street!)<br/>.&gt;)</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  Straight from the Camera.  Dark.  Um.   You have an excellent composition here and lovely colors as well. (And I''m a sucker for those rolled haybales that seem to flow so nicely into the countryside) A Critique Club member is supposed to suggest ways to improve your image, and yes, I caught that note that you work 12-hour shifts.   But still, it''s the lighting that needs to be improved for this image to work perfectly.  Many photographers wait hours for that perfect moment that will make their image The Perfect One.  So without post-processing and without the ability to wait for the perfect moment, well, you did pretty darn well with this very nice image. I''d love to see you revisit this image someday in the right light.  You''ll have a winner, for sure.  (And hey, it''s right across the street!).>)', '2006-07-14 20:59:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(2623812, 356818, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>Well, your commentors gave it to you straight.  This very nice image suffered from "blob in the foreground."  You accidentally created a barrier that made it hard to get at the beauty of that pink flower and flower stem in the middle ground.  <br/> <br/>I tend to forget sometimes that it''s okay to do a little groundskeeping.  If only with a clothspin (or the like) to keep intrusive vegetation out of the way of the subject.<br/> <br/>I''d probably suggest a little more focus on that pretty pink flower, either by use of a tripod or waiting for any wind to die down for that split second you need to make it sharper.  <br/> <br/>And finally, (I know,enough already!) you might try cropping out that partial blue flower on the left.  This would concentrate the viewers attention on that lovely pink flower that I think you want as your focal point.  See what you think.  Is it better?  Or not?<br/> <br/>Still, I''m glad to have seen this image.  Not only does it make me nostalgic for what I think may be peonies, but it makes me see this lovely garden spot through your eyes.  Thank you!<br/> <br/>Alice</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  Well, your commentors gave it to you straight.  This very nice image suffered from "blob in the foreground."  You accidentally created a barrier that made it hard to get at the beauty of that pink flower and flower stem in the middle ground.   I tend to forget sometimes that it''s okay to do a little groundskeeping.  If only with a clothspin (or the like) to keep intrusive vegetation out of the way of the subject. I''d probably suggest a little more focus on that pretty pink flower, either by use of a tripod or waiting for any wind to die down for that split second you need to make it sharper.   And finally, (I know,enough already!) you might try cropping out that partial blue flower on the left.  This would concentrate the viewers attention on that lovely pink flower that I think you want as your focal point.  See what you think.  Is it better?  Or not? Still, I''m glad to have seen this image.  Not only does it make me nostalgic for what I think may be peonies, but it makes me see this lovely garden spot through your eyes.  Thank you! Alice', '2006-07-14 23:53:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(2623881, 356173, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>Charming, charming little girl.  No wonder you wanted to enter this sweet image in competition.  <br/> <br/>Well, the job of the Critique Club is to suggest how to improve your image, so I''m here to give you some thoughts on how to make this little girl stand out as a major attraction.<br/> <br/>Your commentors have already mentioned the lamp backlighting.  I''ll mention, in passing, the OOF canister in the foreground.  And maybe the furniture in the left background.  Unfortunate distractions.<br/> <br/>In my art classes, our instructors frequently talk about how to tell a story, mostly by making a composition simple.  In this image, I would translate that advice to:  A prettily dressed little girl, a tea cup, a sugar bowl, a nice tablecloth.  an appealing smile.  a plain background.   And, yes, the close-up pose you chose.  (Um, this is not easy to do.) <br/> <br/>The image you selected is certainly keepsake material.  She and you will surely cherish it for years to come.  <br/> <br/>And I wish you continued success at DPC.  It looks as if you are having loads of fun with the group.<br/> <br/>Alice</td>', 'greetings from the Critique Club  Charming, charming little girl.  No wonder you wanted to enter this sweet image in competition.   Well, the job of the Critique Club is to suggest how to improve your image, so I''m here to give you some thoughts on how to make this little girl stand out as a major attraction. Your commentors have already mentioned the lamp backlighting.  I''ll mention, in passing, the OOF canister in the foreground.  And maybe the furniture in the left background.  Unfortunate distractions. In my art classes, our instructors frequently talk about how to tell a story, mostly by making a composition simple.  In this image, I would translate that advice to:  A prettily dressed little girl, a tea cup, a sugar bowl, a nice tablecloth.  an appealing smile.  a plain background.   And, yes, the close-up pose you chose.  (Um, this is not easy to do.)  The image you selected is certainly keepsake material.  She and you will surely cherish it for years to come.   And I wish you continued success at DPC.  It looks as if you are having loads of fun with the group. Alice', '2006-07-15 00:11:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(2624029, 357089, 29011, '<td valign="top">That''s a grea shot. Looks a lot like a robber fly aka bee killer. I saw one for the first time myself this past week, attacking a bee no less.<br/> <br/>Edit:<br/>Yep. I looked it up, this one looks like the "Southern Bee-killer"<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-07-15 00:30:24.</i></td>', 'That''s a grea shot. Looks a lot like a robber fly aka bee killer. I saw one for the first time myself this past week, attacking a bee no less. Edit:Yep. I looked it up, this one looks like the "Southern Bee-killer"', '2006-07-15 01:28:10.000000', '2006-07-15 01:30:24.000000', 0),
